2025-05-01 03:40:32 - Thread-1567 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage  

races.year : identify the season we are working on.  
races.race_id : join‐key linking a race to the two “standings” tables.  
races.round : lets us pick the last (max) round of every year to get end‑of‑season standings.  
driver_standings.race_id : join to races so we know what year the standing belongs to.  
driver_standings.driver_id : points scorer (to be turned into a full name).  
driver_standings.points : championship points the driver had after that race.  
driver_standings.position : the champion should have value 1 at the final race.  
drivers.driver_id / drivers.forename / drivers.surname : build the full name, e.g.  
  full_name = drivers.forename || ' ' || drivers.surname.  
drivers_ext.full_name : already concatenated full name (if you prefer).  
constructor_standings.constructor_id : points scoring constructor.  
constructor_standings.points / constructor_standings.position : analogous to the driver table.  
constructors.constructor_id / constructors.name : constructor’s team name.  

Typical conditions we are likely to need  

• Join driver_standings … ON driver_standings.race_id = races.race_id  
• Pick only the last race of each year:  
  WHERE races.round = (SELECT MAX(r2.round)  
                       FROM races AS r2  
                       WHERE r2.year = races.year)  
• Champion / Constructors’ Champion:   
  driver_standings.position = 1  
  constructor_standings.position = 1  

Below are 10 exploratory queries (from simplest to more complex, all capped at 20 rows) that can be run to examine the data and finally obtain the season champion driver + constructor.  

```sql
-- 1. Show the first 20 seasons we have
SELECT DISTINCT "year"
FROM "races"
ORDER BY "year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at a sample of races per year with their round number
SELECT "race_id", "year", "round", "name"
FROM "races"
ORDER BY "year", "round"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect driver standings for one sample race
SELECT *
FROM "driver_standings"
WHERE "race_id" = 18                -- sample race_id shown in the catalogue
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Bring in the season (year) for those driver standings
SELECT ds."race_id", r."year", ds."driver_id", ds."points", ds."position"
FROM "driver_standings" AS ds
JOIN "races"            AS r  ON r."race_id" = ds."race_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Show the last (max‑round) race of a few seasons
SELECT r1."year", MAX(r1."round") AS "final_round"
FROM "races" AS r1
GROUP BY r1."year"
ORDER BY r1."year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Driver standings only for the final race of each season (whoever is still in the table)
SELECT DISTINCT r."year", ds."driver_id", ds."points", ds."position"
FROM "driver_standings" AS ds
JOIN "races"            AS r  ON r."race_id" = ds."race_id"
WHERE r."round" = (
      SELECT MAX(r2."round")
      FROM "races" AS r2
      WHERE r2."year" = r."year")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. From that final race list, keep only the champion driver (position = 1)
SELECT DISTINCT r."year", ds."driver_id", ds."points"
FROM "driver_standings" AS ds
JOIN "races"            AS r  ON r."race_id" = ds."race_id"
WHERE r."round" = (
      SELECT MAX(r2."round") FROM "races" AS r2 WHERE r2."year" = r."year")
  AND ds."position" = 1
ORDER BY r."year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add the full driver name (forename + surname) to the champion list
SELECT DISTINCT r."year",
       d."forename" || ' ' || d."surname" AS "driver_full_name",
       ds."points"
FROM   "driver_standings" AS ds
JOIN   "races"            AS r  ON r."race_id" = ds."race_id"
JOIN   "drivers"          AS d  ON d."driver_id" = ds."driver_id"
WHERE  r."round" = (
       SELECT MAX(r2."round") FROM "races" AS r2 WHERE r2."year" = r."year")
  AND  ds."position" = 1
ORDER  BY r."year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Do the same for constructors – Constructors’ Champion per year
SELECT DISTINCT r."year", c."name" AS "constructor_name", cs."points"
FROM   "constructor_standings" AS cs
JOIN   "races"                 AS r  ON r."race_id" = cs."race_id"
JOIN   "constructors"          AS c  ON c."constructor_id" = cs."constructor_id"
WHERE  r."round" = (
       SELECT MAX(r2."round") FROM "races" AS r2 WHERE r2."year" = r."year")
  AND  cs."position" = 1
ORDER  BY r."year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final combined answer: for each season list both the champion driver and constructor
SELECT  yr."year",
        drv."driver_full_name",
        con."constructor_name"
FROM
  ( /* driver champions */
    SELECT r."year", ds."driver_id"
    FROM   "driver_standings" AS ds
    JOIN   "races"            AS r ON r."race_id" = ds."race_id"
    WHERE  r."round" = (
           SELECT MAX(r2."round") FROM "races" AS r2 WHERE r2."year" = r."year")
      AND ds."position" = 1
  ) AS yr
JOIN  ( /* turn driver_id into full name */
    SELECT d."driver_id",
           d."forename" || ' ' || d."surname" AS "driver_full_name"
    FROM   "drivers" AS d
  ) AS drv   ON drv."driver_id" = yr."driver_id"
JOIN  ( /* constructor champions */
    SELECT r."year", cs."constructor_id", c."name" AS "constructor_name"
    FROM   "constructor_standings" AS cs
    JOIN   "races"                 AS r ON r."race_id" = cs."race_id"
    JOIN   "constructors"          AS c ON c."constructor_id" = cs."constructor_id"
    WHERE  r."round" = (
           SELECT MAX(r2."round") FROM "races" AS r2 WHERE r2."year" = r."year")
      AND  cs."position" = 1
  ) AS con ON con."year" = yr."year"
ORDER BY yr."year"
LIMIT 20;
```

The last query (10) returns, for every year in the database, the full driver name of the World Champion and the name of the Constructors’ Champion.
